 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_k2Mt2ksQ5m4nb6nZ",
   "name": "Acceleration",
   "definition": "know and use the relationship between acceleration, change in velocity and time taken: \na = (v − u) / t"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Acceleration"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Rate of change in velocity"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Acceleration** is defined as the **rate of change in velocity**\n\n  - In other words, it describes how much an object's velocity **changes** every **second**\n- The equation below is used to calculate the average acceleration of an object:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{acceleration}=\\frac{\\mathrm{change} \\mathrm{in} \\mathrm{velocity}}{\\mathrm{time} \\mathrm{taken}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$a=\\frac{\\Deltav}{t}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $a$ = acceleration in metres per second squared (m/s<sup>2</sup>)\n  - $\\Deltav$ = change in velocity in metres per second (m/s)\n  - $t$* *= time taken in seconds (s)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Formula triangle for acceleration, change in velocity and time"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2021/07/5.6.10-Acceleration-Formula-Triangle.png",
   "file": "assets/a253f3b4a9cb-5-6-10-acceleration-formula-triangle.png",
   "alt": "Acceleration Formula Triangle, downloadable IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***To use an equation triangle, simply cover up the value you wish to calculate and the structure of the equation will be revealed***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **change in velocity** is found by the **difference** between the initial and final velocity:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{change} \\mathrm{in} \\mathrm{velocity}=\\mathrm{final} \\mathrm{velocity}-\\mathrm{initial} \\mathrm{velocity}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\Deltav=v-u$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $v$ = final velocity in metres per second (m/s)\n  - $u$ = initial velocity in metres per second (m/s)\n- Therefore, the acceleration, or the rate of change in velocity, equation can be written as:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`a space equals space fraction numerator open parentheses v space minus space u close parentheses over denominator t end fraction`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Speeding up and slowing down"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An object that speeds up is **accelerating**\n- An object that slows down is **decelerating**\n- The **acceleration** of an object can be **positive** or **negative**, depending on whether the object is speeding up or slowing down\n\n  - If an object is **speeding up**, its acceleration is **positive**\n  - If an object is **slowing down**, its acceleration is **negative** (also known as **deceleration**)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Examples of acceleration and deceleration"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2021/07/5.6.10-Acceleration-Examples.png",
   "file": "assets/3f79363e3830-5-6-10-acceleration-examples.png",
   "alt": "Acceleration Examples, downloadable IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***A rocket speeding up (accelerating) and a car slowing down (decelerating)***"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A Japanese bullet train decelerates at a constant rate in a straight line. The velocity of the train decreases from 50 m/s to 42 m/s in 30 seconds.\n\n(a) Calculate the change in velocity of the train.\n\n(b) Calculate the deceleration of the train, and explain how your answer shows the train is slowing down.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Part (a)**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Initial velocity, $u=50m/s$\n- Final velocity, $v=42m/s$\n\n**Step 2: Write the relevant equation**\n\n$\\Deltav=v-u$\n\n**Step 3: Substitute values for final and initial velocity**\n\n$\\Deltav=42-50$\n\n$\\Deltav=-8m/s$\n\n**Part (b)**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Change in velocity, $\\Deltav=-8m/s$\n- Time taken, $t=30s$\n\n**Step 2: Write the relevant equation**\n\n`a space equals space fraction numerator open parentheses v space minus space u close parentheses over denominator t end fraction space equals space fraction numerator increment v over denominator t end fraction`\n\n**Step 3: Substitute the values for change in velocity and time**\n\n$a=\\frac{-8}{30}$\n\n$a=-0.27m/s^{2}$\n\n**Step 4: Interpret the value for deceleration**\n\n- The answer is **negative**, which indicates the train is **slowing down**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "Remember, the units for acceleration are **metres per second squared**, m/s<sup>2</sup>\n\nIn other words, acceleration measures how much the velocity (in m/s) changes every second, m/s/s."
  }
 ]
}